WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.116
Objects in memory cannot simply be copied onto a
network and expected to retain their

00:00:05.116 --> 00:00:10.654
meaning in another process. The receiver may use
another language, object layout, or machine

00:00:10.654 --> 00:00:15.469
architecture. Serialization converts values into
a defined representation, while

00:00:15.469 --> 00:00:18.539
deserialization reconstructs useful values from
it.

00:00:18.539 --> 00:00:24.106
JSON works well for combinations of objects,
arrays, strings, numbers, booleans, and null,

00:00:24.106 --> 00:00:29.612
but domain types still need conventions. The
diagram shows two different client languages

00:00:29.612 --> 00:00:35.303
meeting at one wire representation. A timestamp
needs a timezone convention, a large numeric

00:00:35.303 --> 00:00:40.500
identifier may need a string representation, and
an absent field must have a meaning

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:45.511
distinct from an explicitly supplied null when
the API requires that distinction.

00:00:45.539 --> 00:00:50.471
References make serialization more interesting
than printing nested values. Suppose two

00:00:50.471 --> 00:00:55.630
people share one hometown object. Embedding the
complete hometown under both people repeats

00:00:55.630 --> 00:00:58.804
data and can obscure that they refer to the same
entity.

00:00:59.039 --> 00:01:04.702
A cycle is worse: if Alice references Ben and
Ben references Alice, recursively expanding

00:01:04.702 --> 00:01:10.556
both objects never reaches a natural end. Assign
stable identifiers and serialize a table of

00:01:10.556 --> 00:01:13.420
objects plus those identifiers as references.

00:01:13.539 --> 00:01:18.992
The diagram shows two people pointing to one
shared city record. The receiver can create the

00:01:18.992 --> 00:01:24.445
records first, then resolve links, preserving
sharing without duplicating the entire city or

00:01:24.445 --> 00:01:26.519
endlessly following a cyclic chain.

00:01:26.539 --> 00:01:32.263
Choosing HTTP-based APIs means adopting an
ecosystem as well as a message format. Existing

00:01:32.263 --> 00:01:37.860
libraries handle connections, encryption,
compression, and response parsing; established

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:43.584
proxies and observability tools can inspect
familiar exchanges. For a public catalogue API

00:01:43.584 --> 00:01:49.372
used by many independent clients, that shared
vocabulary can matter more than shaving a few

00:01:49.372 --> 00:01:50.962
bytes from each response.

00:01:51.039 --> 00:01:55.914
The diagram connects different clients through
one documented interface, with common

00:01:55.914 --> 00:02:00.848
operational tooling beside it. These benefits
still require configuration and correct

00:02:00.848 --> 00:02:06.013
semantics. A cache does not become safe merely
because the protocol supports caching, and

00:02:06.013 --> 00:02:09.496
encryption does not replace authentication or
authorization.

00:02:09.539 --> 00:02:14.167
Reuse the infrastructure while explicitly
defining the application's contract.

00:02:14.167 --> 00:02:19.652
Now imagine a high-volume internal call with a
stable schema and many generated clients. A

00:02:19.652 --> 00:02:25.260
schema-driven RPC approach can define operations
and types once, generate language bindings,

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:30.684
and use a compact representation on the wire.
The illustration shows one schema producing

00:02:30.684 --> 00:02:34.037
two compatible client libraries and a server
interface.

00:02:34.167 --> 00:02:39.670
This can reduce repetitive integration work and
catch some mismatches earlier, but it

00:02:39.670 --> 00:02:45.496
introduces schema-evolution rules, generated
artifacts, and tooling that every participant

00:02:45.496 --> 00:02:51.193
must manage. Serialization format and transport
are separate decisions: Protocol Buffers

00:02:51.193 --> 00:02:56.308
does not itself prohibit HTTP, and an RPC system
may use HTTP as its transport.

00:02:56.308 --> 00:03:02.419
Compare complete stacks instead of treating
JSON, HTTP, and RPC as interchangeable

00:03:02.419 --> 00:03:03.239
categories.
